    "title": "Around the state in eight days: Day 8 - by Jeff Morrison",
    "text": "Conclusion of an intermittent series: 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 5½ , 6 , 7\n[trumpet fanfare] W-M-T ! [bomp] Good morn-ing!\n(If you know, you know. If you don’t, I couldn’t find it online. A lost piece of Iowa radio history, perhaps?)\nHousekeeping pounded on my motel room door in Keokuk at 8. I told the desk clerk about it when I checked out and she said, “They know better than that.” When I left and turned on the radio, I heard the familiar Sunday morning jingle above.\nThe final day of my 2015 circumnavigation around Iowa was overcast but otherwise pleasant. The first part of the route was full of awkward backtracks: A return to Oakland Cemetery, the base of the Iowa side of the US 136 bridge across the Mississippi River, a stop at the Chief Keokuk statue , and the Galland school , the first structure in Iowa built for education of European settlers. After I nearly reached Montrose on the Great River Road, a rockslide forced one more backtrack, down County Road X28 to J78 to reach Montrose via the Keokuk airport and US 61.\nThe top of the century-old statue and monument dedicated to Chief Keokuk is pictured in Rand Park in Keokuk, Iowa, Sunday, October 4, 2015. Photo by author. Once I reached and photographed the intersection in downtown Montrose, to my surprise, as I was about to pull out, an old man in a red pickup pulled up beside me. We talked for five to 10 minutes. He waved to his daughter nearby. He said you can get around the rockslide but you’re not supposed to. He said downtown Montrose used to have a bunch of gas stations. Good thing there is still manufacturing here, he said, because without the school lots of towns dry up. I agreed with him.\nI said I was from Traer. He said he had just been up in Belle Plaine for the funeral of a relative of his wife. He’s originally from Victor. He mentioned Clutier and said they used to play “roly-boly” and a place in either Belle Plaine or Victor had a set upstairs. (A “roly-boly” tournament was on the schedule for Chelsea’s quasquicentennial in 1989.)\nThe small rest area on the Great River Road northwest of Montrose has a “Pioneer Trail Memorial” about the Mormons in Nauvoo. ( Here’s a National Park Service version. ) The Mormon Temple is across the river and can be seen from the Iowa side on a good day. “ Nauvoo” can be traced to the Hebrew for “beautiful.”\nFort Madison (the replica fort) is pictured Sunday, October 4, 2015, in Fort Madison (the city), Iowa. The city’s website says: “Historic Old Fort Madison, an active military outpost from 1808 to 1813, acted as a trading post between the US Army and Native Americans. … The fort was the site of Chief Blackhawk’s first battle against U.S. troops, making it the only real War of 1812 battlefield located in the state of Iowa & the first U.S. military cemetery in the upper Midwest.” Photo by author. The Fort Madison rail depot museum was open, although the fort was not. I was there for almost an hour, mostly walking across bridge over tracks to the fort and back. This was the historic Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railway station, and the smaller one across the street was Chicago, Burlington & Quincy.\nI spent some time talking with an old man in overalls who was volunteering there. I said I was from Traer and he asked if I knew a Youel who was a teacher or coach or something. I said I knew a Josh Youel. He mentioned a Youel who played with Nile Kinnick and later got drafted by the Washington Redskins. Later research found a James Youel, originally from Vinton. He was a three-year letterman at Iowa who lived in Fort Madison much of his life after retirement from the NFL. He would die in Traer 4½ years after my trip took place. Josh Youel, the now-former North Tama coach, teacher, and administrator, is one of James Youel’s sons.\nYouel’s obituary has this anecdote:\n“In the fall of 1939, I was 17 years old on the Iowa football team. Freshmen were only allowed to serve as the practice team for varsity scrimmages. My buddy, Tom Farmer, and I were on the sidelines praying our numbers would not be called to play defense against the varsity. As we looked at the Ironmen of 1939, we thought we would be killed because those guys were huge! Farmer’s number was called for the first play and he left the field on a stretcher. My number was called for the second play. On that play, I made a lucky tackle of Nile Kinnick. As I lay on the ground after, I first thought my football career at Iowa was over. But as Kinnick got up and returned to the huddle, he passed by me and said, ‘Nice hit Jim’.”\nThe volunteer at the Fort Museum said someone had brought a bunch of stuff she’d seen headed for a landfill after a woman was taken to a nursing home — a 1972 yearbook and other stuff from the period. He hates to see stuff like that thrown out. Once again I found myself nodding in agreement.\nAt the end of 2021, six years after my trip, the ATSF station returned to active service. It’s now the only depot in Iowa for Amtrak’s Southwest Chief route from Chicago to Los Angeles and had nearly 5,000 riders in fiscal 2024. (There is no ATM, no kiosks, no vending machines, and no Wi-Fi .)\nThe Great River Road through Burlington has been disconnected since the closure of the Cascade Bridge in 2008. That resulted in another backtrack. Burlington received a $1 million grant in May 2023 for restoration of the bridge. However, a $6 million earmark through U.S. Rep. Mariannette Miller-Meeks for the bridge was lost this March when Congress passed a budget resolution that did not include $96 million in funding for various projects in Iowa.\nOfficially, Snake Alley in Burlington has tighter curves and greater total degrees of turn than Lombard Street in San Francisco, which is longer and has more turns. Photo by author taken Sunday, October 4, 2015. I also drove down Snake Alley, the “crookedest street in the world.” I saw other people taking pictures and even a family selfie similar to a picture on the cover of a visitors guide at the time.\nPart of the Great River Road northwest of Toolesboro used to be gravel/dirt until a new concrete road was built to the west in the early 2010s. When this was finished, the Great River Road was completely paved in Iowa. Also along this section is the Port Louisa Wildlife Refuge , although I was unable to visit the visitors center since it was Sunday.\nWhen I reached the Mark Twain Overlook at Muscatine at 3:45 PM, I had completed my loop of Iowa. When I got back to Cedar Rapids, I had covered 1600 miles. That night, it took 25 minutes to copy 1,133 photos from my digital camera and 182 from a backup camera.\nOverall, I visited 17 county seats (including both Keokuk and Fort Madison), 18 incorporated places I had never been before, seven unincorporated places I had never been before, and all three boundary markers (northeast corner, tri-state, old northwest corner of Missouri).\nI think this was a unique idea and a unique trip. It gave me a different structure for seeing parts of the “beautiful land.”\nMaybe if I win Powerball I’ll drive a giant loop around the Lower 48 states.\nBetween Two Rivers is a reader-supported publication. To receive new posts and support my work, consider becoming a free or paid subscriber.\nSubscribe Share\nBetween Two Rivers is a reader-supported publication. To receive new posts and support my work, consider becoming a free or paid subscriber.",
